{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Compare naive RAG and hybrid retrieval+reranking.\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"naive RAG architecture components limitations and failure modes\\\",\\\"hybrid retrieval combining lexical and dense vectors benefits tradeoffs\\\",\\\"reranking techniques cross-encoder vs bi-encoder latency accuracy benchmarks\\\",\\\"empirical comparisons naive RAG versus hybrid retrieval plus reranking performance\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"both\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Comparison table and implementation tradeoffs summary\\\"}\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Compare naive RAG with hybrid retrieval and reranking.\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 0.5, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: Incomplete tool usage: missing tools [ToolCall(\n    name=\"knowledge_search\"\n)]; expected ['web_search', 'knowledge_search'], called ['web_search']. See more details above.\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Explain how RAG reduces but doesn't eliminate hallucinations.\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"mechanisms by which retrieval-augmented generation reduces hallucinations\\\",\\\"root causes of hallucination in LLMs despite retrieval augmentation\\\",\\\"empirical studies evaluating hallucination rates in RAG systems\\\",\\\"best practices and failure modes for minimizing hallucinations in RAG\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"both\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Concise explanation with mechanisms, limitations, and mitigation\\\"}\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"How does RAG reduce hallucinations, and why does it still not eliminate them?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: All expected tools ['web_search', 'knowledge_search'] were called (order not considered).\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Outline RAG pipeline stages from chunking to answer generation.\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"RAG pipeline stages chunking embeddings retrieval reranking generation\\\",\\\"best chunking strategies for retrieval augmented generation\\\",\\\"vector store retrieval methods FAISS Milvus similarity search tradeoffs\\\",\\\"fusion-in-decoder vs retrieve-and-refine RAG architectures prompt construction\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"both\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Ordered stages with brief explanations\\\"}\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Summarize the main stages of a RAG pipeline from chunking to final answer generation.\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 0.5, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: Incomplete tool usage: missing tools [ToolCall(\n    name=\"knowledge_search\"\n)]; expected ['web_search', 'knowledge_search'], called ['web_search']. See more details above.\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Overview\\n- Purpose: Compare \\\"naive RAG\\\" (single-stage dense-vector retrieval used directly as context for an LLM) with \\\"hybrid retrieval + reranking\\\" (multiple retrieval signals \\u2014 dense + sparse/lexical \\u00b1 late-interaction \\u2014 followed by an explicit reranker) and explain tradeoffs, when to use each, and practical notes for production systems.\\n- Sources used: local knowledge base summary on RAG (retrieval-augmented-generation.pdf) and two web resources (Qdrant hybrid search + reranking tutorial; comparison/analysis article on naive RAG vs hybrid + reranking).\\n\\nComparison table\\n\\n| Dimension | Naive RAG (single-stage dense retrieval) | Hybrid retrieval + Reranking |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| Core architecture | Embed docs & queries with one dense encoder, nearest-neighbor (cosine) top-k \\u2192 pass chunks to LLM | Multiple retrieval signals (dense + sparse/BM25, possibly late-interaction) \\u2192 union/top-N \\u2192 reranker (neural cross-encoder or late-interaction such as ColBERT) \\u2192 top-k to LLM |\\n| Retrieval signals | Dense (semantic) only | Dense + sparse (BM25/lexical) \\u00b1 late-interaction; reranker uses deeper pairwise scoring |\\n| Relevance precision | OK for conversational, well-written corpora; often lower precision for technical/IDed info | Higher precision on difficult/technical queries; better at exact-match / structured-language cases |\\n| Hallucination risk | Higher: semantically-close but incorrect chunks cause LLM to hallucinate | Lower: reranker filters out near-miss documents, reducing misleading context |\\n| Robustness to structured/keyword-heavy content (IDs, code, logs, PDFs) | Weak \\u2014 semantic vectors can miss exact tokens/IDs | Stronger \\u2014 BM25 and late-interaction help recover exact matches |\\n| Latency & cost | Lower (single vector search) | Higher: extra sparse search and compute-heavy reranker (cross-encoder) \\u2192 increased CPU/GPU and query latency |\\n| Implementation complexity | Low \\u2014 easier to prototype and maintain | Higher \\u2014 needs orchestration of multiple signals, reranker model, and tuning |\\n| Data / annotation needs | Minimal (only embeddings) | Often needs supervised signals or relevance labels for best reranker performance (but unsupervised heuristics can help) |\\n| Typical pipeline | embed(query) \\u2192 retrieve top-k \\u2192 LLM | embed(query) + BM25 \\u2192 retrieve union top-N \\u2192 rerank (cross-encoder/late-interaction) \\u2192 select top-k \\u2192 LLM |\\n| When it\\u2019s sufficient | Low-stakes Q&A, demos, clean internal wikis, FAQ bots | Production support portals, manuals, compliance, troubleshooting, high-stakes or multi-turn systems |\\n| Scaling considerations | Scales well for high QPS with vector DB | Increased infra: reranker GPU/CPU autoscaling, more storage for multiple embeddings, more indexing steps |\\n| Evaluations to track | Precision@k, downstream QA F1, latency | Same + MRR, reranker accuracy, retrieval/QA F1, hallucination rate |\\n\\nKey information (how the hybrid + reranking components help)\\n- Sparse signals (BM25): capture exact token matches, term frequency & document-length signals that dense vectors miss. Useful for IDs, unique tokens, and PDFs where wording is exact.\\n- Late-interaction (ColBERT-style): computes contextualized per-token interactions between query and document embeddings for precise matching while keeping scalable retrieval properties.\\n- Neural rerankers (cross-encoders): run a scoring model on (query, doc-chunk) pairs that uses full attention to produce highly accurate relevance scores \\u2014 expensive but effective for top candidates.\\n- Retriever training tricks (from local KB): Inverse Cloze Task (ICT) for pretraining retrievers; supervised alignment of retriever with generator by minimizing KL divergence between retriever distribution and generator likelihood (improves end-to-end relevance).\\n- Practical two-stage pattern: retrieve a broad candidate set (dense+BM25 \\u2192 maybe 50\\u2013200 items), then rerank to a small set (5\\u201310) for the LLM. This balances recall and precision while amortizing reranker cost.\\n\\nComparison / details (practical tradeoffs & tuning)\\n- Candidate pool sizing: bigger candidate pools increase chance of including the correct doc but raise reranker cost. Typical practice: retrieve union of top-N from each signal, then rerank to final top-k.\\n- Reranker choices:\\n  - Cross-encoder: best accuracy; expensive; good for high-value queries or low QPS.\\n  - Late-interaction (ColBERT): good precision with better scaling than full cross-encoder.\\n  - Learning-to-rank / pairwise losses: require relevance labels but give greatest precision gains.\\n- When to add BM25: when documents contain keywords/IDs, or when vector-only retrieval returns semantically related but incorrect docs (a common source of hallucination).\\n- Monitoring & metrics: track precision@k, MRR, downstream QA F1/EM, hallucination incidents, and latency; use BEIR-like benchmarks when applicable (local KB mentions BEIR as a standard).\\n- Cost mitigation: run reranker only on top-N pre-filter; use mixed-precision or CPU-optimized models for reranking; cache reranker outputs for repeated queries.\\n\\nKey takeaways (actionable guidance)\\n- Start simple for prototypes: naive RAG is fast to build and often adequate for conversational, low-risk applications.\\n- If you see consistent hallucinations, wrong-but-similar documents, or you work with technical/content-heavy corpora, move to hybrid retrieval + reranking.\\n- Hybrid + rerank is the production pattern for reliability: combine dense semantics with BM25/lexical signals, then apply a strong reranker (cross-encoder or late-interaction) before sending context to the LLM.\\n- Expect increased engineering and infra cost for hybrid + reranking; prioritize it where correctness matters (support systems, compliance, internal knowledge).\\n- Instrument: measure retrieval precision, reranker lift, downstream accuracy, and latency to justify complexity/cost.\\n- Use retriever/reranker training techniques (ICT, supervised alignment) where you can collect labels \\u2014 they pay off in relevance and reduced hallucination.\\n\\nSources\\n- Retrieval-augmented generation (local knowledge base: retrieval-augmented-generation.pdf) \\u2014 background on RAG, retriever pretraining (ICT), supervised retriever optimization, and the role of reranking. [local]\\n- Qdrant \\u2014 \\\"Hybrid Search with Reranking\\\" tutorial (tutorial explanation of dense + sparse + late-interaction + ColBERT and an implementation guide). [web] https://qdrant.tech/documentation/tutorials-search-engineering/reranking-hybrid-search/\\n- \\\"Naive RAG vs. Hybrid Search + Reranking\\\" (practical comparison / lessons learned). [web] https://www.ebartan.dev/naive-rag-vs-hybrid-search-reranking\\n\\nIf you want, I can:\\n- Sketch a sample two-stage pipeline (exact retrieval/rerank parameters and component suggestions), or\\n- Propose evaluation metrics and a small experiment plan to quantify reranker benefit on your corpus. Which would you prefer?\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Compare naive RAG with hybrid retrieval and reranking.\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"naive RAG vs hybrid retrieval\\\",\\\"reranking techniques RAG\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"knowledge_base\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Comparison table and key takeaways\\\"}\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: All expected tools ['knowledge_search'] were called (order not considered).\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Overview\\n- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) reduces hallucinations by grounding a generative LLM\\u2019s output in retrieved external evidence (documents, snippets, or knowledge base passages) so the model is more likely to produce answers supported by source text instead of inventing facts.\\n\\nKey information \\u2014 how RAG reduces hallucinations (mechanisms)\\n1. Grounding / conditioning on real evidence\\n   - The LLM is given retrieved passages as part of its context; generation is thereby constrained to language and facts present in those passages, lowering the model\\u2019s incentive/need to invent unsupported facts.\\n2. Retrieval-as-memory for up-to-date / domain facts\\n   - Instead of relying only on the LLM\\u2019s pretraining, the system supplies current, domain-specific facts at inference time, reducing errors from stale or missing knowledge.\\n3. Narrowing the solution space with relevant context\\n   - A good retriever returns a small set of high-relevance chunks (top-k). The generator can focus on reconciling and synthesizing those chunks, which reduces freeform speculation.\\n4. Reranking and filtering\\n   - RAG systems commonly include a reranker or verification step to surface higher-quality evidence and drop irrelevant/noisy documents that could mislead the generator.\\n5. Explicit citation and provenance\\n   - Returning sources or inline citations lets downstream users (or automated verifiers) check and reject unsupported claims \\u2014 this increases transparency and enables detection of hallucinations.\\n6. Hybrid retrieval and augmentation strategies\\n   - Combining dense (vector) retrieval with lexical (BM25) or metadata filters helps retrieve both semantically relevant and exact-match evidence, improving coverage and reducing missed facts that would otherwise be hallucinated.\\n7. System design choices that help:\\n   - Small, well-trained retrievers can allow use of smaller LLMs while keeping accuracy (reduces generation errors and cost).\\n   - Prompting to force \\u201canswer only from provided sources\\u201d or to admit uncertainty when evidence is insufficient reduces unsupported generation.\\n\\nLimitations & failure modes (why RAG is not a complete cure)\\n- The LLM can still \\u201challucinate around\\u201d retrieved content: it may misinterpret a retrieved passage, overgeneralize from partial evidence, or synthesize claims not present in sources.\\n- Bad retrieval = bad grounding: if the retriever misses relevant documents or returns misleading/low-quality sources, RAG can still produce incorrect outputs.\\n- Conflicting sources: when retrieved documents disagree, the model may pick one arbitrarily or synthesize an incorrect reconciliation.\\n- Context truncation: long sources must be chunked; important context can be lost or split across chunks, causing errors.\\n- RAG reduces but does not eliminate the need for model-level checks (calibration, abstention, fact verification).\\n\\nPractical best practices to maximize hallucination reduction\\n- Use high-quality, well-indexed source sets and hybrid retrieval (dense + lexical).\\n- Rerank candidate documents and include only the most relevant passages in the prompt.\\n- Provide provenance/citations in outputs and enable automated fact-checking against sources.\\n- Prompt the model to cite the source span for each factual claim and to say \\u201cI don\\u2019t know\\u201d when evidence is insufficient.\\n- Add verification steps (secondary LLM verifier or symbolic checks) to confirm claims extracted from sources.\\n- Monitor and tune retriever recall for target tasks; small retrievers trained for the domain can be especially effective.\\n- For structured outputs, condition generation tightly on retrieved structured examples and constraints (this has been shown to cut hallucinations substantially).\\n\\nKey takeaways\\n- RAG substantially reduces hallucinations by grounding generation in retrieved evidence, improving precision and enabling provenance.\\n- Its effectiveness depends strongly on retrieval quality, reranking, prompt design, and verification; RAG reduces but does not eliminate hallucination risk.\\n- Combine RAG with citation, verification, and abstention strategies for best real-world results.\\n\\nSources\\n- retrieval-augmented-generation.pdf (local knowledge base) \\u2014 discussion of RAG grounding, evidence/citation benefits, limitations [local]\\n- \\u201cReducing hallucination in structured outputs via Retrieval-Augmented Generation\\u201d (arXiv 2024) \\u2014 empirical description of how RAG reduced hallucination and allowed smaller retriever + LLM deployments [web]\\n- Stanford HAI article \\u201cReduce AI Hallucinations With This Neat Software Trick\\u201d \\u2014 overview commentary on retrieval-based grounding to reduce hallucination (background/context) [web]\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"How does RAG reduce hallucinations?\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"RAG hallucination reduction\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"web\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Summary with sources\\\"}\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: All expected tools ['web_search', 'read_url'] were called (order not considered).\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Overview\\nA Retrieval\\u2011Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline is a production architecture that lets a large language model (LLM) answer queries using external, up-to-date documents. The pipeline performs a sequence of data and retrieval steps so the LLM can generate grounded, domain\\u2011specific responses rather than rely only on its training data.\\n\\nStep\\u2011by\\u2011step summary (main stages)\\n\\n1. Data ingestion\\n- Collect and centralize sources: documents, web pages, PDFs, databases, logs, APIs.\\n- Maintain provenance/metadata (source, date, doc id) for later citation and freshness checks.\\nPractical tip: automate refresh and change detection for frequently updated sources.\\n\\n2. Preprocessing / cleaning\\n- Normalize text (remove boilerplate, fix encoding), extract text from PDFs/HTML, remove duplicates.\\n- Preserve structure (headings, tables, images encoded or described) via metadata.\\nPractical tip: use libraries (Unstructured, BeautifulSoup) and keep original file references.\\n\\n3. Chunking / segmentation\\n- Split long documents into smaller chunks (tokens / sentences / paragraphs) suitable for embedding and retrieval.\\n- Decide chunk size and overlap (tradeoff: larger chunks preserve context; smaller chunks increase retrieval granularity).\\nPractical tip: prefer semantic or heading-aware chunking; include some overlap (e.g., 50\\u2013200 token windows) to avoid boundary cuts.\\n\\n4. Embedding creation\\n- Convert each chunk to a vector embedding using a chosen embedding model (semantic vector representation).\\n- Store embeddings alongside chunk text and metadata.\\nPractical tip: choose embedding model matching your domain and retrieval needs; consider dimensionality vs latency tradeoffs.\\n\\n5. Indexing (vector DB)\\n- Insert embeddings into a vector index/database (FAISS, Milvus, Pinecone, Weaviate, Redis Vector, etc.).\\n- Add auxiliary indexes for metadata and/or full\\u2011text search (for hybrid search).\\nPractical tip: index time is a good place to add filters, deduplication, and metadata tags for later scoped retrieval.\\n\\n6. Retrieval (ANN / search)\\n- For an incoming query, compute its embedding and perform approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search to fetch top candidate chunks.\\n- Optionally combine with sparse methods (BM25) for hybrid retrieval to improve recall.\\nPractical tip: tune k (returned results) to balance context length and relevance; use metadata filters to scope search (e.g., date, doc type).\\n\\n7. Re\\u2011ranking / filtering\\n- Re-rank retrieved candidates using a cross-encoder or heuristic scoring to surface the most relevant passages.\\n- Remove low-quality or contradictory chunks; apply answerability checks.\\nPractical tip: small transformer re-ranker improves precision when vector recall returns noisy hits.\\n\\n8. Context assembly / prompt construction\\n- Assemble the final prompt: include user query, selected retrieved chunks (with citations/metadata), and system instructions or templates.\\n- Decide strategy: concatenate top chunks, use summarization of chunks, or stepwise retrieval (retrieval + generation loops).\\nPractical tip: limit prompt token budget; consider compressing context (extractive summary) or using retrieval\\u2011into\\u2011tooling (multi\\u2011turn retrieval).\\n\\n9. Generation (LLM)\\n- Pass the assembled prompt to the LLM to generate the answer.\\n- Use generation controls (temperature, max_tokens, stop sequences) and instruct the model to cite sources or refrain from inventing facts.\\nPractical tip: require the model to quote sources and provide provenance to reduce hallucinations.\\n\\n10. Post\\u2011processing and validation\\n- Normalize and format the LLM output, attach citations, and run simple validators (QA checks, factual verifiers, or rule-based filters).\\n- Optionally run an answer verification step (secondary retrieval, fact\\u2011checking model) before exposing to users.\\nPractical tip: add confidence/scoring and an \\u201cI may be wrong\\u201d UI when unsure.\\n\\n11. Orchestration, caching, and monitoring\\n- Orchestrate the pipeline (APIs, workers, queues); cache frequent queries and retrieved contexts to reduce latency.\\n- Monitor retrieval quality, latency, drift, and user feedback. Track freshness and re\\u2011indexing needs.\\nPractical tip: log retrieval traces linking user queries to chunks for debugging and continuous improvement.\\n\\n12. Evaluation and governance\\n- Evaluate with retrieval and generation metrics (recall, precision, BLEU/ROUGE for summaries, human eval for factuality).\\n- Implement access controls, data privacy, and explainability (citation display, redaction where needed).\\nPractical tip: use benchmarks (BEIR, NQ) for retrieval tuning and periodic manual review for hallucination rates.\\n\\nOptional: Common alternatives / implementation choices\\n- Retrieval style: RAG\\u2011Sequence (retrieved docs influence decoding per token) vs RAG\\u2011Fusion or simple concat\\u2011and\\u2011generate. Most production systems use \\u201cretrieve + concat + generate.\\u201d\\n- Relevance models: vector only vs hybrid (vector + sparse BM25) vs neural re\\u2011rankers.\\n- Chunking: fixed window vs semantic/heading-aware vs sentence/paragraph boundaries.\\n- Index choices: FAISS/Milvus for self\\u2011hosted; Pinecone/Weaviate/Redis Vector for managed services.\\n\\nKey takeaways\\n- RAG is an end\\u2011to\\u2011end pipeline, not just \\u201cretrieval + LLM.\\u201d Good ingestion, chunking, indexing, and re\\u2011ranking are critical to reliability.\\n- Chunking and embedding quality strongly influence retrieval relevance; mis\\u2011chunking is a common source of errors.\\n- Use hybrid retrieval and a lightweight re\\u2011ranker to improve precision while maintaining recall.\\n- Prompt construction and provenance (citations) are the main levers to reduce hallucinations and increase user trust.\\n- Monitor, refresh, and evaluate continuously\\u2014data freshness and retrieval accuracy degrade without maintenance.\\n\\nSources\\n- Retrieval\\u2011augmented generation (local knowledge base: retrieval-augmented-generation.pdf) [local]\\n- \\u201cStep-by-step Guide to Building a RAG (Retrieval\\u2011Augmented Generation) Pipeline,\\u201d Nimbleway (web) \\u2014 https://www.nimbleway.com/blog/rag-pipeline-guide [web]\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"{\\\"goal\\\":\\\"Summarize the main stages of a RAG pipeline.\\\",\\\"search_queries\\\":[\\\"RAG pipeline stages chunking embedding retrieval generation\\\"],\\\"sources_to_check\\\":[\\\"both\\\"],\\\"output_format\\\":\\\"Step-by-step summary\\\"}\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: All expected tools ['knowledge_search', 'web_search', 'read_url'] were called (order not considered).\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"knowledge_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"web_search\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"read_url\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": null, \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"User request: Compare naive RAG with hybrid retrieval.\\n\\nResearch findings:\\n# RAG Approaches Comparison\\nHybrid retrieval outperforms naive RAG for technical queries.\\n\\nCritique verdict: APPROVE\\n\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Tool Correctness", "threshold": 0.5, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "[\n\t Tool Calling Reason: All expected tools ['save_report'] were called (order not considered).\n\t Tool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria\n]\n", "strictMode": false, "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Expected Tools:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"save_report\"\n    )\n] \n \nTools Called:\n[\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"plan\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"research\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"critique\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"research\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"critique\"\n    ),\n    ToolCall(\n        name=\"save_report\"\n    )\n] \n \nAvailable Tools: [] \n \nTool Selection Score: 1.0 \n \nTool Selection Reason: No available tools were provided to assess tool selection criteria"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.5, "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true, "evaluation_params": []}}]}, "{\"actual_output\": \"\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"A strong overview should distinguish between inference-time adaptation and post-training adaptation. On the inference side, prompt engineering and few-shot prompting adapt behavior without changing model weights, while tool use and RAG extend the model with external actions and external knowledge. On the training side, instruction fine-tuning teaches the model to follow user requests, and RLHF further shapes behavior toward responses humans prefer, such as answers that are more helpful, truthful, and harmless. The answer should note that these methods are complementary rather than mutually exclusive.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Give me a compact but complete overview of modern LLM adaptation methods.\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"This request is outside the intended knowledge domain of the system, which is centered on research topics such as RAG, LLMs, retrieval, and LangChain-style agent workflows. A good response should say that the question is not grounded in the local corpus and is not an appropriate target for this benchmark. It should briefly redirect the user toward a weather service or a general assistant rather than pretending to answer from the research knowledge base.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What's the weather in Paris tomorrow and what should I wear?\", \"retrieval_context\": null}": {"cached_metrics_data": []}}}